@elalemanyo If you look at the existing `Dockerfile` you'll see what the basic version requires; we have a few additional dependencies in `saas/Dockerfile` that we use in production.

Many of these dependencies are surely already in the rails base container, and I'm totally not even sure the OSS `Dockerfile` is complete (the saas file is probably a better resouroce from that perspective). But I was asking specifically about the additional dependencies that deal with attachment handling: libvips, mupdf, libreoffice-*.

Probably not showstoppers for basic development, so maybe it's OK! But also it feels very weird to have the set of system libraries being declared differently in multiple places.

Thanks, @vixen24! Instead of adjusting the margins, it would be preferable to bump the z-index when an element has focus. That way the spacing is never dependent on the style/weight of the focus ring. Something like this:

```css
.popup__btn {
  &:focus-visible {
    z-index: 1;
  }
}
```

@jeremy that's a red herring. Claude thinks its a race condition but it isn't. 

The challenge is only fetched when the button or conditional mediation is triggered. On sign in there is no way to trigger both since the sign in button is hidden. This race condition also happens on registration, mostly on Windows, but there it's just the one button. So it can't be a race condition. If you press it on this it will start theorizing that it's Chrome's fault because of its cookie protections, but it's actually just a race condition in how Chrome handles cookies.

I removed the cookie altogether https://github.com/basecamp/fizzy/pull/2751 and now rely solely on fetching the challenge via API call and verify it by its signature.

